[My live AWS Cloud Account (ap-south-1)]
       │
       ├── Provisions VPC networks, Docker Host, and EKS Cluster.
       └── Saves the persistent data back into your secure S3 backend bucket.

Step-by-Step Implementation Blueprint :  

Phase1: High-Availability Infrastructure Provisioning (Terraform)
engineered a highly available network topology inside AWS using Terraform to ensure repeatable deployments:
Network Isolation: A custom Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) segmented into both public and private subnets across multiple Availability Zones (AZs).EKS Architecture: The Amazon 
EKS control plane is configured to manage worker node groups locked down inside private subnets, shielding production workloads from direct public internet exposure.

Phase 2: Compute Workstation & Security GatewaysCI Workstation:
Terraform stands up a dedicated Ubuntu EC2 instance within the VPC to serve as a centralized local build host. 
Static Code Analysis: SonarQube is installed on this host to capture immediate code vulnerabilities, scanning repository files directly against automated enterprise security
standards.

Phase 3: Container Engineering & Edge Routing. 
Optimized Dockerization: The Flask application (app.py) is compiled using a multi-stage Dockerfile with a minimal python:3.11-slim base image to trim down file size and 
reduce attack vectors.
Sanity Testing: Containers are validated on the build host by binding internal application runtime processes (0.0.0.0:5000) to external host interfaces and tweaking stateful 
AWS Security Groups to safely accept public HTTP traffic.

Phase 4: Cluster Orchestration via GitHub ActionsAutomated Workflow: 
Upon a code check-in to the main branch, the GitHub Action workflow spins up an ephemeral virtual machine, pulls down deployment manifests, updates Kubernetes cluster context 
via AWS CLI (aws eks update-kubeconfig), and commands the EKS cluster to orchestrate stateless container.

ERRORS: 

1.OIDC Authentication Failure | Chicken-and-egg paradox: The GitHub workflow tried assuming an IAM Role that hadn't been built yet by the unexecuted Terraform scripts. 
| Refactored initial handshake using securely stored AWS Access Key ID and Secret Access Key repository secrets to bootstrap the system.|

2.Terraform Local Profile Crash (Exit Code 1) | Hardcoded profile = "default" forced Terraform to hunt for a physical file path (~/.aws/credentials) absent on cloud-hosted
runners. | Stripped the local profile parameter from the provider block, allowing environmental variables to feed configuration naturally. |  

3.Missing Terraform Working Directory | GitHub Action runners search root folders; configuration files were nested inside a isolated /terraform directory.
| Configured step definitions explicitly utilizing the working-directory: ./terraform flag. |

4.Backend Storage Dependency Failure | Chicken-and-egg paradox: Terraform required an S3 bucket to maintain state history before it could even run its own init commands. 
| Manually instantiated the core S3 tracking bucket directly inside the AWS Console beforehand to anchor the initialization stage. |

5.JSON String Corruption (Exit Code 3) | Runner linters and regex engines misidentified raw IAM JSON strings (like ://amazonaws.com) as web URLs and forced formatting
corruptions (://). | Migrated away from risky jsonencode text structures to standard native aws_iam_policy_document data sources. | 

6.Docker Build Missing Context | Pipeline configurations set build execution limits to ./app, but the project Dockerfile resided natively at the root folder level.
| Pointed the docker configuration back to universal workspace boundaries with context: .. |

